1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,050
Lesson 9.4.

2
00:00:02,430 --> 00:00:04,340
A cost model for diffusion.

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:08,343
To decide whether diffusion
pays off on a given

4
00:00:08,343 --> 00:00:10,989
hardware, an explicit
cost model is needed.

5
00:00:11,369 --> 00:00:15,136
The central idea is simple: a
diffusion model takes T steps,

6
00:00:15,136 --> 00:00:18,651
and in each it processes every
position in the sequence.

7
00:00:19,031 --> 00:00:21,535
The energy per token is then T times the

8
00:00:21,535 --> 00:00:24,414
energy of processing one
position in one step.

9
00:00:24,794 --> 00:00:30,576
Today we put numbers for a GPU and for
a spatial TSU, with stated assumptions.

10
00:00:31,683 --> 00:00:34,630
On a GPU, processing one position with an

11
00:00:34,630 --> 00:00:37,791
N-parameter model costs
about 2N operations.

12
00:00:38,171 --> 00:00:41,725
With the specification of
a modern GPU, about 0.7

13
00:00:41,725 --> 00:00:44,915
picojoules per operation
at full throughput.

14
00:00:45,295 --> 00:00:49,297
But the GPU rarely runs at
full throughput: the useful

15
00:00:49,297 --> 00:00:52,928
fraction is the MFU, the
model FLOPs utilization.

16
00:00:53,308 --> 00:00:59,209
The energy per position and
step is e_pos = 2N e_FLOP / MFU.

17
00:01:00,317 --> 00:01:05,030
Autoregressive generation
produces one token per pass and is

18
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:09,901
memory-bound: with small batches,
the MFU can be five percent.

19
00:01:10,281 --> 00:01:14,470
Diffusion processes all
positions in parallel and can

20
00:01:14,470 --> 00:01:18,660
reach an MFU near fifty
percent, but repeats T times.

21
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:22,703
For a billion-parameter model,
under these assumptions,

22
00:01:22,703 --> 00:01:27,433
autoregressive decoding costs about
twenty-eight millijoules per token.

23
00:01:27,813 --> 00:01:31,383
Diffusion costs twenty-three
with eight steps, ninety-one with

24
00:01:31,383 --> 00:01:35,415
thirty-two, and three hundred sixty-two
with one hundred twenty-eight.

25
00:01:36,517 --> 00:01:42,611
The break-even point is T* = MFU_dif /
MFU_AR: with these numbers, ten steps.

26
00:01:42,991 --> 00:01:46,816
Below it, diffusion wins in
energy; above it, it loses.

27
00:01:47,196 --> 00:01:49,907
That is why on GPUs
everything hinges on reducing

28
00:01:49,907 --> 00:01:52,232
steps with good samplers and distillation.

29
00:01:52,612 --> 00:01:57,041
The number is only as good as the
assumptions: the real MFU depends

30
00:01:57,041 --> 00:02:00,875
on batch, length and implementation,
and must be measured.

31
00:02:01,983 --> 00:02:06,797
In a spatial TSU, each variable has
its own circuit, so there is no MFU

32
00:02:06,797 --> 00:02:11,406
to lose: the energy per position
and step is the number of cells per

33
00:02:11,406 --> 00:02:15,338
position, times the sweeps,
times the energy of each cell.

34
00:02:15,718 --> 00:02:19,529
In the DTM of the previous lesson,
about twenty nanojoules per

35
00:02:19,529 --> 00:02:23,094
seven-hundred-eighty-four-pixel
image is about twenty-five

36
00:02:23,094 --> 00:02:27,211
picojoules per pixel, or about
three picojoules per pixel and step.

37
00:02:27,591 --> 00:02:30,463
Diffusion stops penalizing
steps so heavily,

38
00:02:30,463 --> 00:02:33,400
because each step is
cheap physical dynamics.

39
00:02:34,517 --> 00:02:38,699
One last lever: not all steps
need the same precision.

40
00:02:39,079 --> 00:02:42,797
The first ones, when the sample
is almost pure noise, tolerate

41
00:02:42,797 --> 00:02:46,396
fewer bits; the last ones, which
fix the details, need more.

42
00:02:46,776 --> 00:02:50,662
If cost per step grows with
bits, running the first half of

43
00:02:50,662 --> 00:02:54,614
thirty-two steps at half the
bits saves twenty-five percent.

44
00:02:54,994 --> 00:02:57,741
It is an assumption to validate
against sample quality.

45
00:02:58,850 --> 00:03:02,856
Use this model to report joules
per token with all assumptions

46
00:03:02,856 --> 00:03:06,992
explicit, compute the break-even
number of steps before choosing

47
00:03:06,992 --> 00:03:10,352
diffusion on a GPU, and
estimate the energy on TSUs.

48
00:03:10,732 --> 00:03:13,445
In lab thirteen you
will sweep the number of

49
00:03:13,445 --> 00:03:16,281
steps and report quality
and projected energy.

50
00:03:17,383 --> 00:03:21,171
Common mistakes: assuming
the GPU's peak throughput.

51
00:03:21,551 --> 00:03:25,124
Comparing diffusion and
autoregression at different qualities.

52
00:03:25,504 --> 00:03:28,597
Forgetting that diffusion
repeats each position T times.

53
00:03:28,977 --> 00:03:33,402
And mixing estimated TSU energies
with measured GPU energies.

54
00:03:34,517 --> 00:03:37,505
In summary: the energy
per token of diffusion is

55
00:03:37,505 --> 00:03:40,057
T times the energy per position and step.

56
00:03:40,437 --> 00:03:45,097
On a GPU, diffusion gains utilization
over autoregression but pays for

57
00:03:45,097 --> 00:03:49,491
each step, with a break-even near
ten steps under our assumptions.

58
00:03:49,871 --> 00:03:52,407
On a spatial TSU the energy per step is

59
00:03:52,407 --> 00:03:55,073
small and does not depend on utilization.

60
00:03:55,453 --> 00:03:58,947
And precision schedules
save on the noisy steps.

61
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:05,026
To practice: compute the energy per
position for a seven-billion model.

62
00:04:05,406 --> 00:04:07,682
Compute the break-even
with another utilization.

63
00:04:08,062 --> 00:04:10,906
Compute the energy per position on a TSU.

64
00:04:11,286 --> 00:04:14,596
And explain why the first
steps tolerate fewer bits.
